1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,210
On the second of July, 1944,

2
00:00:03,230 --> 00:00:05,231
closed American ambulances drove through

3
00:00:05,251 --> 00:00:08,061
the Normandy countryside toward the front line.

4
00:00:08,081 --> 00:00:10,082
Inside them were German women.

5
00:00:10,102 --> 00:00:13,315
They were prisoners of the United States Army.

6
00:00:13,335 --> 00:00:15,336
Before the evening was over,

7
00:00:15,356 --> 00:00:18,590
they would be back on the German side.

8
00:00:19,875 --> 00:00:22,279
A short cease-fire had been arranged over

9
00:00:22,299 --> 00:00:22,971
the radio.

10
00:00:22,991 --> 00:00:26,087
One of the Americans who arranged it was a

11
00:00:26,107 --> 00:00:27,818
young captain named Quentin Roosevelt,

12
00:00:27,838 --> 00:00:29,549
the grandson of a president.

13
00:00:29,569 --> 00:00:31,627
At about six in the evening,

14
00:00:31,647 --> 00:00:34,396
the women were taken through the lines at

15
00:00:34,416 --> 00:00:35,915
a village called Caumont.

16
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:39,346
Online, there are many stories about

17
00:00:39,366 --> 00:00:41,109
German nurses in American hands,

18
00:00:41,129 --> 00:00:43,224
most of them with conversations nobody

19
00:00:43,244 --> 00:00:43,930
wrote down.

20
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:46,750
This is the documented story of the first

21
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:48,866
German nurses the American army captured

22
00:00:48,886 --> 00:00:52,040
in France, and of the rule that made the

23
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:53,470
army give them back.

24
00:00:55,125 --> 00:00:57,614
After D-Day, the Allies needed a

25
00:00:57,634 --> 00:00:58,450
deep-water port.

26
00:00:58,470 --> 00:01:00,585
The nearest was Cherbourg,

27
00:01:00,605 --> 00:01:02,537
at the tip of the Cotentin peninsula.

28
00:01:02,557 --> 00:01:05,558
American troops attacked the city on the

29
00:01:05,578 --> 00:01:06,853
twenty-second of June.

30
00:01:06,873 --> 00:01:10,265
Four days later they took Fort du Roule,

31
00:01:10,285 --> 00:01:12,941
above the harbor, and captured the German

32
00:01:12,961 --> 00:01:15,900
commander, General Karl-Wilhelm von Schlieben.

33
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:19,026
The arsenal held out three more days.

34
00:01:19,046 --> 00:01:21,472
It surrendered on the twenty-ninth of June

35
00:01:21,492 --> 00:01:23,569
after two American officers bluffed its

36
00:01:23,589 --> 00:01:25,665
commander, making their force sound far

37
00:01:25,685 --> 00:01:27,063
bigger than it was.

38
00:01:27,083 --> 00:01:29,509
Before they gave up, the Germans had

39
00:01:29,529 --> 00:01:31,256
wrecked and mined the port.

40
00:01:31,276 --> 00:01:34,052
It would be weeks before the first ships

41
00:01:34,072 --> 00:01:35,120
could use it.

42
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:38,497
Tens of thousands of Germans became

43
00:01:38,517 --> 00:01:41,143
prisoners in the fight for the peninsula.

44
00:01:41,163 --> 00:01:43,410
And in an underground hospital near

45
00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:45,918
Cherbourg, the Americans found prisoners

46
00:01:45,938 --> 00:01:49,638
of a kind they had not taken before in France.

47
00:01:49,658 --> 00:01:51,870
An Army photographer's caption called them

48
00:01:51,890 --> 00:01:54,846
the first German Army nurses to fall into

49
00:01:54,866 --> 00:01:55,666
American hands.

50
00:01:56,950 --> 00:02:01,123
Some of them were nurses of the German Red Cross.

51
00:02:01,143 --> 00:02:04,059
Others were auxiliaries of the German navy.

52
00:02:04,079 --> 00:02:07,413
The Allied papers called all of them nurses.

53
00:02:07,433 --> 00:02:09,530
Some called them Nazi nurses.

54
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:13,932
The question was what to do with them.

55
00:02:13,952 --> 00:02:16,534
The answer was in a treaty signed in

56
00:02:16,554 --> 00:02:20,310
Geneva in July 1929, a convention on the

57
00:02:20,330 --> 00:02:22,990
wounded and sick of armies in the field.

58
00:02:23,010 --> 00:02:25,000
The United States had signed it.

59
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:27,345
It protected the wounded, and the people

60
00:02:27,365 --> 00:02:29,710
whose job was to care for them.

61
00:02:30,925 --> 00:02:32,892
Its ninth article covered medical staff

62
00:02:32,912 --> 00:02:33,885
who were captured.

63
00:02:33,905 --> 00:02:36,865
If they fall into the hands of the enemy

64
00:02:36,885 --> 00:02:39,865
they shall not be treated as prisoners of war.

65
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:43,601
Its twelfth article said they could not be

66
00:02:43,621 --> 00:02:45,763
detained, and what had to happen next.

67
00:02:45,783 --> 00:02:48,233
They shall be sent back to the belligerent

68
00:02:48,253 --> 00:02:50,704
to whose service they are attached as soon

69
00:02:50,724 --> 00:02:53,483
as a way is open for their return and

70
00:02:53,503 --> 00:02:54,430
military exigencies permit.

71
00:02:55,750 --> 00:02:57,532
Until then, the treaty said,

72
00:02:57,552 --> 00:03:00,055
they should keep working, caring for the

73
00:03:00,075 --> 00:03:01,857
wounded of their own side.

74
00:03:01,877 --> 00:03:04,380
Captured medical staff could be kept as

75
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,543
long as prisoners needed their care.

76
00:03:06,563 --> 00:03:10,147
When that need ended, they were to be sent home.

77
00:03:10,167 --> 00:03:12,330
Red Cross nurses were covered too.

78
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,646
On the second of July, nine of the women

79
00:03:16,666 --> 00:03:18,423
were brought to the forty-fifth Evacuation

80
00:03:18,443 --> 00:03:20,760
Hospital, near the village of La Cambe,

81
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:21,492
in Normandy.

82
00:03:21,512 --> 00:03:24,787
They did not know what was going to happen

83
00:03:24,807 --> 00:03:25,519
to them.

84
00:03:25,539 --> 00:03:28,081
Only after they arrived were they told

85
00:03:28,101 --> 00:03:29,912
that they were going back.

86
00:03:29,932 --> 00:03:31,742
According to the hospital's account,

87
00:03:31,762 --> 00:03:32,860
they were overjoyed.

88
00:03:34,050 --> 00:03:35,840
Before they left, the hospital's

89
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:38,012
commanding officer walked them through it.

90
00:03:38,032 --> 00:03:40,547
They saw its supplies and its equipment.

91
00:03:40,567 --> 00:03:42,719
They talked to German patients and

92
00:03:42,739 --> 00:03:44,891
prisoners who were being treated there.

93
00:03:44,911 --> 00:03:47,788
What they wanted to know most was how

94
00:03:47,808 --> 00:03:50,322
wounded Germans were being cared for in

95
00:03:50,342 --> 00:03:51,790
England and in France.

96
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:56,108
The American staff had a hope of their own.

97
00:03:56,128 --> 00:03:58,385
They hoped the women would remember what

98
00:03:58,405 --> 00:04:00,663
they had seen, and treat American wounded

99
00:04:00,683 --> 00:04:02,960
in German hands with the same kindness.

100
00:04:04,275 --> 00:04:06,838
If you want more prisoner of war stories

101
00:04:06,858 --> 00:04:09,555
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

102
00:04:09,575 --> 00:04:10,024
Frontlines.

103
00:04:10,044 --> 00:04:12,855
It helps us keep finding them.

104
00:04:14,475 --> 00:04:17,351
Then they waited, while a cease-fire was

105
00:04:17,371 --> 00:04:19,006
negotiated with the enemy.

106
00:04:19,026 --> 00:04:21,535
Closed ambulances took them to Balleroy,

107
00:04:21,955 --> 00:04:23,813
a village close to the front.

108
00:04:23,833 --> 00:04:26,005
There they waited about two hours more,

109
00:04:26,025 --> 00:04:28,197
while the last arrangements were made with

110
00:04:28,217 --> 00:04:30,095
German officers on the other side.

111
00:04:31,375 --> 00:04:34,945
The cease-fire was agreed over the radio.

112
00:04:34,965 --> 00:04:37,509
The account names three officers.

113
00:04:37,529 --> 00:04:40,650
Captain Fred Ghercke commanded an American

114
00:04:40,670 --> 00:04:42,857
team that interrogated prisoners.

115
00:04:42,877 --> 00:04:46,168
Captain Quentin Roosevelt served with the

116
00:04:46,188 --> 00:04:47,823
First Infantry Division.

117
00:04:47,843 --> 00:04:50,059
Major Heeren commanded the reconnaissance

118
00:04:50,079 --> 00:04:52,935
battalion of the German second Panzer Division.

119
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:57,120
An Army photographer was at Balleroy that day.

120
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:59,360
One of his pictures shows an American

121
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:01,600
ambulance parked in front of a house,

122
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:04,480
four of the women, and a group of American

123
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:05,440
soldiers around them.

124
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:07,360
At about six in the evening,

125
00:05:07,380 --> 00:05:10,950
the women were taken through the lines at Caumont.

126
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:14,181
Quentin Roosevelt was twenty-four years old.

127
00:05:14,201 --> 00:05:16,691
He was the grandson of President Theodore

128
00:05:16,711 --> 00:05:19,560
Roosevelt, and he was named after an uncle

129
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:22,787
who had been killed in the First World War.

130
00:05:22,807 --> 00:05:26,014
On D-Day he had landed at Omaha Beach with

131
00:05:26,034 --> 00:05:27,110
the first wave.

132
00:05:28,425 --> 00:05:30,133
His father, General Theodore Roosevelt

133
00:05:30,153 --> 00:05:32,897
Junior, had come ashore at Utah Beach that

134
00:05:32,917 --> 00:05:33,588
same morning.

135
00:05:33,608 --> 00:05:36,352
Ten days after the truce, on the twelfth

136
00:05:36,372 --> 00:05:39,116
of July, the general died of a heart

137
00:05:39,136 --> 00:05:40,153
attack in Normandy.

138
00:05:40,173 --> 00:05:43,263
Father and son had spent part of that day

139
00:05:43,283 --> 00:05:44,665
in a long conversation.

140
00:05:46,150 --> 00:05:48,390
Three days later, an Army photographer

141
00:05:48,410 --> 00:05:51,402
took a picture of Quentin marching in his

142
00:05:51,422 --> 00:05:52,532
father's funeral procession.

143
00:05:52,552 --> 00:05:55,545
The general was later awarded the Medal of

144
00:05:55,565 --> 00:05:58,558
Honor for what he did on Utah Beach.

145
00:05:58,578 --> 00:06:00,064
Quentin survived the war.

146
00:06:00,084 --> 00:06:04,590
He died in a plane crash near Hong Kong in 1948.

147
00:06:04,610 --> 00:06:07,275
He was twenty-nine.

148
00:06:08,375 --> 00:06:10,259
A week after the first crossing,

149
00:06:10,279 --> 00:06:11,212
it happened again.

150
00:06:11,232 --> 00:06:13,751
On the ninth of July, more women were

151
00:06:13,771 --> 00:06:16,095
driven down the same road between Balleroy

152
00:06:16,115 --> 00:06:18,051
and Caumont and handed back.

153
00:06:18,071 --> 00:06:20,723
Sixteen German nurses and navy auxiliaries

154
00:06:20,743 --> 00:06:23,415
were returned in the two crossings.

155
00:06:24,825 --> 00:06:27,347
An Army photo dated the eighth of July

156
00:06:27,367 --> 00:06:29,889
shows seven of them sitting on a bench,

157
00:06:29,909 --> 00:06:31,478
waiting, with an American military

158
00:06:31,498 --> 00:06:33,405
policeman behind them holding a rifle.

159
00:06:34,775 --> 00:06:36,653
The story reached the papers.

160
00:06:36,673 --> 00:06:38,551
Yank, the Army's own magazine,

161
00:06:38,571 --> 00:06:41,209
told it at the end of July,

162
00:06:41,229 --> 00:06:43,107
and an Associated Press correspondent

163
00:06:43,127 --> 00:06:45,385
wrote about it, according to the

164
00:06:45,405 --> 00:06:46,144
hospital's history.

165
00:06:46,164 --> 00:06:48,422
But the Army's photographs went through

166
00:06:48,442 --> 00:06:49,561
the censor first.

167
00:06:49,581 --> 00:06:52,598
The caption on one of them was only

168
00:06:52,618 --> 00:06:54,496
cleared for publication without any

169
00:06:54,516 --> 00:06:58,305
restriction in May 1945, after Germany had

170
00:06:58,325 --> 00:06:59,125
surrendered.

171
00:07:00,450 --> 00:07:02,724
What the sixteen women told anyone about

172
00:07:02,744 --> 00:07:04,689
the American hospital, once they were

173
00:07:04,709 --> 00:07:06,328
home, we have not found.

174
00:07:06,348 --> 00:07:08,949
The only record of that day is the

175
00:07:08,969 --> 00:07:11,590
American one, and the hope written into it.

176
00:07:12,675 --> 00:07:15,677
The rule they were sent home under was not

177
00:07:15,697 --> 00:07:16,348
a favor.

178
00:07:16,368 --> 00:07:18,027
It was in the treaty.

179
00:07:18,047 --> 00:07:20,713
In a war that broke almost every rule,

180
00:07:20,733 --> 00:07:22,728
on two summer evenings in Normandy,

181
00:07:22,748 --> 00:07:23,755
this one held.

182
00:07:25,050 --> 00:07:27,226
If your family has a story about a nurse

183
00:07:27,246 --> 00:07:28,690
in the war, on either side,

184
00:07:28,710 --> 00:07:29,930
tell us in the comments.

185
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:33,790
Caumont was liberated by the First

186
00:07:33,810 --> 00:07:35,650
Infantry Division on the thirteenth of

187
00:07:35,670 --> 00:07:38,580
June, 1944.

188
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:41,178
Today a memorial in the village marks it,

189
00:07:41,198 --> 00:07:43,777
and a plaque on the church remembers the

190
00:07:43,797 --> 00:07:45,726
division that Quentin Roosevelt served in.

191
00:07:45,746 --> 00:07:49,290
The road from Balleroy is a quiet country road.

192
00:07:49,310 --> 00:07:53,242
Twice in July 1944, the war stopped on it

193
00:07:53,262 --> 00:07:54,860
long enough for sixteen women to cross.

